Types of Authentication
Password - less sophisticated
Token - This is usually a device or token that temporarily proves your identity. These include One-Time Passwords (OTPs) sent via SMS or generated by an app.
Biometrics - Voice, retina, fingerprint etc.
Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) or Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) 

Common Access Control Models
- Discretionary Access Control DAC systems: access is granted by the owner of the object, usually the person who created the file or resource. 
This model is flexible and simple to implement, but it carries risks. Users can unintentionally share sensitive data by granting permissions too broadly or overlooking security implications.
eg. A shared Google Doc where the owner sets who can view or edit the file.
- Mandatory Access Control MAC: a strict, hierarchical model often used in environments where security policies must be enforced without exception, like military or classified government systems. 
Access is based on labels; both users and data are assigned classification levels. Only users with sufficient clearance can access certain resources. 
It’s highly secure but difficult to adapt to fast-changing needs. eg. A classified document that only employees with “Top Secret” clearance can open. 
- Role-based Access Control RBAC: RBAC provides a scalable and maintainable middle ground.
Instead of granting permissions to individual users, administrators create roles (like “Editor” or “Manager”) and assign permissions to those roles. 
Users are then assigned roles based on their responsibilities. It aligns well with organizational structure, making it the most widely adopted model in enterprise systems.
eg.  A project management app where roles like “Admin,” “Contributor,” and “Viewer” determine what users can do.
  The core of RBAC is users, permissions, and roles
In a typical RBAC implementation, administrators assign users to roles, and roles already carry the permissions.
In complex or regulated environments, RBAC is often extended with features from Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC). 
This allows for policies that consider more than just roles, like time of access, location, or device type.
Example: An editor may have permission to publish articles, but only during business hours and from a company-managed device.

Federated Identity
To address the limitations of traditional login systems, many modern applications use federated identity, delegating authentication to trusted identity providers (IdPs) like Google or Microsoft.
Instead of handling passwords directly, your app receives tokens from the IdP.  These tokens confirm who the user is and what they’re allowed to do.
A trio of protocols work together to manage identity and access across systems:
- OAuth2.0 - Handles delegated access. OAuth is a protocol that lets an application access certain resources on your behalf without needing your password.
For example, you let a photo-printing app access selected photos from your Google account.
- Open ID Connect OIDC - Built on OAuth 2.0, it adds identity verification. It allows an application to verify who you are and sign you in, often using an identity provider such as Google. 
- JSON web tokens - They act as secure, self-contained proof of authentication, like a digital passport that lets users move safely between systems.
It's often digitally signed so the recipient can verify it hasn't been tampered with. A signed JWT is not necessarily encrypted, so its contents may be readable.

OAuth 2.0 different grant types: OAuth isn’t one-size-fits-all. Each grant type serves a specific use case, whether you're logging in on a laptop, approving access on a smart TV, or connecting backend services.
Authorization Code - Most secure. Used for server-side apps (like websites)
Implicit - Legacy flow. Not recommended due to security issues.
Client Credentials - Used when one service talks to another, no user is involved.
Device code - Designed for devices without browsers (like TVs).
Resource owner password client - The app collects the user’s password directly, discouraged due to high risk and poor separation of trust

OpenID Connect- this is not a replacement for OAuth but an added feature. Imagine OAuth as the protocol that gets you a visitor badge to a building. 
OpenID Connect is the feature that prints your name and photo on the badge. It gives context to the token, and it links it to a verifiable user.
OpenID Connect achieves this through the ID Token, a specially crafted JSON Web Token (JWT) that carries information about the authenticated user.
This token is signed by the identity provider (IdP) and contains “claims” such as a unique identifier, name, email, when the token arrives, and when it expires, etc.

PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) is a security mechanism used with OAuth 2.0 to protect the login process, especially for mobile apps and applications running in a browser.
 - A password is created; a scrambled copy is given to the other app alongside a token, so even when token is stolen, the password will not match.
PKCE protects the authorization-code exchange from interception attacks 

OAUTH - RESOURCE OWNER, 
CLIENT APP -  It sends the user through the login process, obtains or receives tokens, and uses them when making requests.
AUTHORIZATION SERVER - It handles the authorization process and issues tokens after the required checks succeed.
RESOURCE SERVER - It receives requests from the client, checks the access token and permissions, and returns the requested data if access is allowed.
